      "essay": "Jio Platforms is targeting a fundraise of about Rs 30,200 crore at a price band of Rs 1,065 to Rs 1,119 per share, valuing the company at up to Rs 10.3 lakh crore, or roughly $106 billion. That is below the approximately $131 billion expectation reported in June, when the draft papers were filed, a cut of about 19%. Reuters linked the lower pricing to pressure on Indian equities from an oil-price shock and geopolitical uncertainty.\n\nThe issue is entirely fresh equity, up to 27 crore new shares, about 2.93% of post-issue capital, with no offer-for-sale component. Reuters reported that up to Rs 27,500 crore of net proceeds will go to repay or prepay borrowings of Reliance Jio Infocomm. The anchor book is expected to open on October 19, public subscription from October 21 to 23, and listing around October 28. All of these remain reported plans, not confirmed terms.\n\nAt this size the offering would surpass Hyundai Motor India's Rs 27,859 crore IPO from October 2024, currently India's largest. Jio Platforms reported FY26 revenue from operations of Rs 1,46,885 crore, up 14.6%, EBITDA of Rs 76,255 crore, up 18.8%, and profit after tax of Rs 30,049 crore, up 15.1%. Reliance Jio Infocomm served 524.4 million customers as of March 2026. For anyone holding the topic, the lower valuation is the fact that matters: it sets the entry price and the demand test.",
