    {
      "headline": "Existing Jio backers hold, some seek bigger stakes",
      "essay": "Jio's existing investor base, which includes Meta, Google, Saudi Arabia's Public Investment Fund, KKR, Silver Lake, Mubadala, General Atlantic, Abu Dhabi Investment Authority and TPG, does not plan to sell in the IPO, according to people familiar with the matter. Some are seeking to increase their exposure instead. Meta's affiliate Jaadhu Holdings is the largest external holder at 9.98%, followed by Google International at 7.73%.\n\nAkash Ambani and Isha Ambani have completed roadshows across the Americas, Europe and Southeast Asia, and are now meeting domestic mutual funds and institutions. Jio is being pitched as a technology company rather than a telecom business. Reliance Industries will keep about 66.4% after listing, its first public offering in three decades.\n\nMotilal Oswal said Reliance's current share price already reflects an estimated 18% to 36% holding company discount on its Jio stake, and called concerns about telecom allocations shifting from Bharti Airtel to Jio overblown, since Jio will start with limited free float. The brokerage expects a smartphone tariff rise of about 15% in December 2026. Reported investor interest is not the same as confirmed demand, which only shows up at subscription.",
      "sources": [3, 4]
    }
  ]
}

