  "view": "Flipkart's 13th Big Billion Days opened strong: 6.3 million concurrent users in a single minute, over 287 million early-access sessions, and new customers up 34%. Quick commerce is the standout, with Flipkart Minutes orders up 3.5x and new customers up 11x, including an iPhone 17 delivered in 3.4 minutes. Tier 3 towns drove nearly half of first-hour visits and about 60% of demand. The numbers are company-reported and cover only the opening, so treat them as momentum, not the full sale.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Flipkart Big Billion Days opens with record traffic",
      "essay": "Flipkart's 13th Big Billion Days opened with 6.3 million concurrent users in a single minute, and more than six times as many Indians shopped on the platform in the first hour as in a regular hour. Early access, which started at midnight on 8 October for eligible members, drew over 287 million sessions. New customers rose 34%.\n\nSmaller markets did much of the work. Nearly half of first-hour visits came from Tier 3 towns and smaller locations, and non-metro areas contributed about 60% of demand. Large appliance sales grew 30% year on year in the opening hour, tablets 73%, and beauty 42%. At peak, Flipkart sold more than 1,600 men's perfumes per minute. One in four mobile customers used exchange, and one in three chose no-cost EMI.\n\nAmazon's Great Indian Festival, which began on 8 October, reported product orders up 100% year on year and customers ordering from every pin code. The two companies measure different periods and categories, so the figures are not a direct comparison. For Flipkart, the opening shows demand is broad and financing-led, but it is still only the first hour of a long sale.",
