      "essay": "Flipkart's 13th Big Billion Days opened with 6.3 million concurrent users in a single minute during the first hour, more than six times the usual hourly shopper traffic. New customer additions grew 34% in that hour, and the Early Access phase that began at midnight on October 8 for Flipkart Plus, Black and co-branded card members logged more than 287 million sessions.\n\nDemand came heavily from smaller cities. Nearly one in every 15 Indian online shoppers visited Flipkart in the first hour, with Tier 3 towns and beyond accounting for close to half the visits and non-metro markets about 60% of demand. Sardulgarh, Panskura, Sheikhpura and Kishaneanj were named among the fastest-growing markets. Affordability shaped phone buying: one in four customers used product exchange and one in three took no-cost EMI, while entry-level 5G phones sold at last year's prices drew strong purchases.\n\nCategory numbers were led by large appliances, up 30% year-on-year in the first hour on air conditioners, refrigerators and washing machines, and tablets, up 73%. Beauty rose 42%, with more than 1,600 units of men's perfume sold per minute at peak. Flipkart Minutes, the quick-commerce arm, saw order volumes rise 3.5 times over regular days and new customers grow 11-fold; during Early Access an iPhone 17 reached a Delhi customer in 3.4 minutes. On the seller side, 51% of transacting sellers booked twice their regular-day business on Early Access day, and MSME sellers in Tier 2 markets and beyond grew 2.9-fold.",
      "sources": [2, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "iPhone 17 and Vivo T5x headline Flipkart's phone discounts",
      "essay": "Flipkart's sale is running heavy phone discounts against Amazon's Great Indian Festival. The Apple iPhone 17, whose base model was priced at Rs 99,900 after a hike, is selling for as low as Rs 76,999 during the sale, and the two platforms are being compared head to head on deals. The iPhone 17 carries a 6.3-inch Super Retina XDR OLED panel with a 120Hz refresh rate, a 48MP main camera, a 48MP ultrawide and an 18MP selfie shooter, a 3nm A19 chip and up to 512GB of storage.\n\nOn the value end, the Vivo T5x 5G launched at Rs 45,999 and is listed at Rs 27,999, with the 6GB RAM and 128GB storage variant going as low as Rs 24,999 during the sale. Buyers can stack up to Rs 4,000 in bank discount and exchange benefits of up to Rs 22,425, depending on eligibility. The phone's pitch is a 7,200mAh battery with 44W charging, a MediaTek Dimensity 7400-Turbo chipset and a 6.76-inch 120Hz display.\n\nThe pattern is the same as the broader sale: deep upfront discounts, bank offers and exchange values pull buyers into an upgrade. For Flipkart, phones remain the volume and traffic driver, and the premium-to-budget spread is being covered at both ends.",
      "sources": [3, 4]
    },
    {
      "headline": "QLED TV price cuts land on Flipkart and Amazon",
      "essay": "Festive discounts have hit 55-inch QLED smart TVs, with prices starting at Rs 31,999 across Amazon and Flipkart. Flipkart carries two of the five deals highlighted: the Acerpure Nitro 55-inch QLED at Rs 37,999 against a listed Rs 80,990, offering 4K, a 120Hz refresh rate, Dolby Vision, HDR10 and a 50W Dolby Atmos audio system with Google TV and HDMI 2.1; and the Motorola 55-inch QLED Google TV at Rs 31,999, down from Rs 66,999, with 4K, 120Hz, Dolby Vision, 50W Dolby Atmos, Google TV, built-in Google Gemini, Chromecast and 32GB storage.\n\nThe other three run on Amazon: Samsung's 55-inch Neo QLED 4K Vision AI at Rs 67,990 from an MRP of Rs 99,900, Xiaomi's 55-inch Smartchoice FX Pro QLED at Rs 35,499 from Rs 62,999, and TCL's 55-inch QD-Mini LED Google TV at Rs 54,990 from Rs 89,990. Listed prices can change, and bank discounts, exchange offers and availability affect the final amount.\n\nLarge appliances were already the fastest-growing big-ticket category in the sale's first hour, and TV promotions feed the same festive replacement cycle that is driving air conditioners, refrigerators and washing machines.",
      "sources": [5]
    }
  ]
}

topic|Bitcoin|https://www.coindesk.com/m.text:
{
  "stance": "neutral",
  "view": "Mixed flow. Order books for bitcoin and ether are deeper than before the Oct 2025 crash, which is a real structural improvement. But spot volume is still about two-thirds below crash-week levels, altcoin liquidity keeps eroding, and leverage remains a live risk. Ownership data is long-term bullish but not new today. Net: neutral.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Bitcoin and ether order books rebuilt a year after $19 billion crash",
      "essay": "A year after the largest liquidation event in crypto history, bitcoin and ether order books hold more resting liquidity than on crash day or at the start of this year. Bitcoin depth within 1% of the price is about $11.7 million, roughly 75% more than a year earlier and up from about $9 million at the start of 2026 and about $6.9 million at the start of 2025. Ether's tightest band has more than doubled since crash day to about $4.2 million.\n\nThe improvement is real capital, not a price effect. Bitcoin trades about one-third cheaper than before the crash, so a deeper dollar book means market makers committed more, not that coins are worth less. CoinDesk researcher Saksham Diwan said the majors' deepening is real capital. The rebuilt books got a test this week: as the market sold off, bitcoin's 1% depth fell about 12% between Oct 7 and Oct 8.\n\nAltcoins and spot trading did not recover. Altcoin depth at 5% from the price is down about a third since the start of 2025 to around $2 million. Weekly spot volume averaged about $279 billion over the four weeks to Sept 27, nearly two-thirds below the $801 billion traded in the crash week. CoinDesk's Joshua de Vos expects the divergence to persist into next year as majors keep dominating institutional interest.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Leverage and crowded positioning still pose crash risk a year later",
      "essay": "The conditions that caused the October 2025 crash have not gone away. Bitcoin fell from around $122,000 to $105,000 in minutes on Oct 10, 2025, triggering roughly $19 billion in liquidations. Coming in, open interest was near historic highs and traders were piled into bullish bets on the four-year cycle.\n\nMark Connors of Risk Dimensions said the move was driven by derivatives, not onchain data, and that perpetual futures remain a major part of crypto trading. Exchanges have strong incentives to keep offering leveraged products. Chris Sullivan of Hyperion Decimus advises avoiding leverage and watching open interest, funding rates and sentiment for extremes.\n\nConnors also said the four-year halving cycle has changed and can no longer be relied on for as much signal. He sees economic and political forces playing a larger role. His conclusion: the market bent but did not break, and traders are now more attentive to market structure.",
      "sources": [5]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Individuals hold two-thirds of bitcoin, big accumulators hold minority",
      "essay": "Individuals hold about 14 million BTC, roughly two-thirds of the 21 million that will ever exist, according to River's 2026 adoption report. Satoshi Nakamoto's untouched stash is estimated at 968,000 coins, or 4.6% of the total, though a September 2026 Cointelegraph investigation put the range from just under 900,000 to about 1.2 million BTC. Another estimated 1.6 million coins are permanently lost.\n\nAmong businesses, Strategy holds 848,000 BTC, about 4% of the total, while every other company combined holds about 3.9%. ETFs as a group held just 7.1% of supply, and governments about 2.5% as of early October 2026. In 2025, individual holders sold a net 696,000 BTC while businesses bought 489,000, funds and ETFs 205,000, and governments 135,000. River projects those three groups could own about 11.7 million BTC, or 56% of the supply cap, by 2036.\n\nNo single holder can dictate price or protocol policy, since owning coins confers no voting power. The groups most likely to want more bitcoin own a minority today, which means they must bid for coins from current holders.",
      "sources": [3, 6]
    }
  ]
}

