    {
      "headline": "Strategy's Bitcoin buying slows as it spends on preferred stock",
      "essay": "Strategy bought 334 Bitcoin for $28.7 million between Sept. 28 and Oct. 4, bringing its holdings to exactly 848,000 BTC, according to an Oct. 5 8-K. Over the same period it repurchased about 1.77 million shares of its STRC preferred stock for $176.3 million, more than six times its weekly Bitcoin buying budget. Chair Michael Saylor teased more buying on X with \"There's always room for more orange,\" but any official filing won't likely come before Tuesday because federal offices are closed Monday for Columbus Day.\n\nAnalyst Shanaka Anslem Perera noted Strategy raised $5.41 billion from new common shares in the third quarter and ended with 1,666 more BTC than at the end of June. SEC filings allocate 9.5% of that raise directly to Bitcoin purchases, with the rest going to a dollar reserve, cash, dividends and preferred-share buybacks. Perera told Cointelegraph that raising capital does not necessarily mean buying Bitcoin, and preferred shares have claims ahead of common stock.\n\nFor holders, the signal is smaller than the headline. Strategy is still accumulating, but the pace has slowed and much of its capital is going to balance-sheet items rather than BTC.",
