  "stance": "bullish",
  "view": "HAL is expanding engine output and moving Tejas Mk1A toward delivery. The AL-31FP line rises from 30 to 50 units a year, and a second Shakti line targets 100 engines annually by 2028-29. Tejas Mk1A has 29 airframes built but awaits software certification. The main risk is execution on these timelines.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "HAL to boost AL-31FP and Shakti engine output",
      "essay": "HAL will raise annual production of the Russian-origin AL-31FP engine from 30 to 50 units and set up a second Shakti helicopter engine line with capacity up to 100 engines a year. The MoD announced the moves on 6 October 2026 during Defence Minister Rajnath Singh's visit to HAL's Engine Division at Koraput, where he saw delivery of the 2,000th engine made there.\n\nThe AL-31FP powers the IAF's Su-30MKI fleet. Under a September 2024 order worth INR260 billion (US$3 billion), HAL is to deliver 240 engines by 2032. It has delivered 63 engines against a target of 60 in the programme's second year, and the remaining 177 will now be built at 50 a year. HAL expects indigenous content to reach 63% by the end and average over 54% across the programme. Singh said the first engine arrived ahead of schedule despite supply chain and raw material problems.\n\nThe Shakti engine, an Indian derivative of Safran's Ardiden 1H1 made under licence in Bengaluru, powers the Dhruv and Prachand helicopters. Its second line is due fully established by 2028-29. For HAL, the expansions lift engine availability for the IAF and deepen domestic manufacturing.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "headline": "MIDHANI supplies superalloys for HAL engine programmes",
      "essay": "MIDHANI is developing single-crystal superalloys and other high-temperature metals for HAL's aero-engine projects. These materials are designed to withstand the heat and stress inside modern engines. The work aims to cut India's reliance on foreign suppliers for strategic aerospace materials.\n\nThe collaboration was highlighted at HAL's 2,000th aero-engine rollout at Koraput on 6 October 2026, an AL-31FP for the Su-30MKI fleet. The event marked six decades of propulsion manufacturing since the division was set up in 1964. MIDHANI Chairman and Managing Director Dr S.V.S. Narayana Murty said domestic development of critical materials is fundamental to self-reliance in aero-engine production. For HAL, a local superalloy supply chain supports its engine output plans.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Tejas Mk1A nears certification; Mk2 first flight targeted for Q3 2027",
      "essay": "HAL has built 29 Tejas Mk1A fighters with all hardware modifications, but software integration and validation remain. The AESA radar software from Israel's ELTA, the electronic warfare suite, and weapon launcher software are still being tested. HAL is working with the IAF, the Aeronautical Development Agency, and quality regulators to finish certification and start deliveries.\n\nBeyond the Mk1A, the Tejas Mk2 is a heavier 4.5-generation multirole fighter meant to replace the Mirage 2000, Jaguar, and MiG-29 fleets. It will use a GE F414 engine and carry an internal radar jammer, infrared search and track, and network-centric warfare tools. HAL expects first engine ground runs by March 2027 and a maiden flight in the third quarter of 2027. The AMCA stealth fighter programme remains under ADA direction, with no specific milestones given.\n\nHAL is also pushing into civil aviation. It is in technical and commercial talks with Russia's United Aircraft Corporation for licensed production of the SJ-100 regional aircraft, following an October 2025 memorandum and a January 2026 showcase at Wings India. The goal is a facility in India assembling around 200 aircraft over the next decade. HAL's civil portfolio includes the Dhruv NG helicopter, the Civil LUH, and the Hindustan-228, with four Hindustan-228s operating in Guyana. It is investing in a Nashik MRO hub and an A320 C-check facility with Airbus. For HAL, the test is meeting certification deadlines and delivering on time.",
