      "headline": "Over 1,000 ex-Flipkart staff press Walmart on ESOP liquidity",
      "essay": "More than 1,000 former employees of the Flipkart group have signed a collective appeal to Walmart and Flipkart's board, seeking liquidity for vested employee stock options. The representation, dated October 10, covers ex-staff of Flipkart, Myntra, Cleartrip, Shopsy, Supermoney and ANS, and lists over 500 signatures within 24 hours of circulation. It follows an earlier letter from former senior executives including ex-Myntra CEO Mukesh Bansal, former CBO Ankit Nagori, ex-CTOs Amod Malviya and Ravi Garikipati, former CPO Mekin Maheshwari and former VP Anuj Chowdhary.\n\nThe petition makes three asks: extend the post-exit exercise window for vested options until the next liquidity event, include former employees in the planned $2 billion buyback on terms comparable to current employees rather than a token allocation, and appoint a point of contact for a representative group. Walmart's legal counsel said former employees will be considered during the IPO when it happens, with no firm commitment. The petition says the absence of a confirmed IPO timeline could leave former employees waiting several years.\n\nMore than 30,000 current and former employees stand to receive around $4 billion from ESOP buybacks Walmart is yet to facilitate, with former employees accounting for roughly half. Flipkart's IPO could be delayed by another one to two years, and the company is targeting a valuation of about $50 billion. Flipkart was valued at about $38.2 billion in July's buyback, up 6 per cent from $36 billion in May 2024. For the topic, the dispute keeps attention on the listing timeline and on how Walmart treats the equity it handed out.",
      "sources": [1, 2, 3]
