    {
      "headline": "Jio Platforms IPO set for October 21, targeting $106 billion valuation",
      "essay": "Jio Platforms will open its IPO for public subscription on October 21 and close on October 23, with a listing expected on October 28. The offering targets a valuation of roughly $106 billion, which would make it India's largest-ever IPO. The issue size is estimated at $3.8 billion, consisting of fresh shares partly intended to repay borrowings.\n\nThe valuation falls short of earlier speculative estimates of $130 billion to $170 billion, and that gap has weighed on sentiment. Reliance Industries shares have dropped 6% over the past month, with analysts pointing to the lower-than-expected valuation as a factor. Jefferies raised its model portfolio weight for Reliance to 7.50% as the IPO approaches, according to Moneycontrol.\n\nJio Platforms' financials show improvement: net leverage fell from 0.88 times in fiscal 2024 to 0.36 times in fiscal 2026, while revenue from operations rose from INR 110.0 billion to INR 147.0 billion and profit after tax increased from INR 21.423 billion to INR 30.049 billion. Reliance held 66.43% of Jio Platforms as of March 31, 2026. For investors, the IPO provides a defined valuation event, but the lower target and recent stock weakness suggest caution.",
      "sources": [1, 4, 5]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Musk pledges affordable Starlink in India ahead of Jio IPO",
      "essay": "Elon Musk said Starlink will need to price its satellite internet service affordably in India, where mobile data costs about 8 cents per gigabyte. \"Obviously, if Starlink is too expensive in India, no one will buy it, so SpaceX will need to make pricing affordable,\" Musk wrote on X on Saturday. He added that competition would benefit Indian consumers and that Ambani's profits might decline but \"he is not going out of business.\"\n\nThe comments escalate a public feud with Mukesh Ambani just days before Jio Platforms' IPO. Musk also referred to Ambani sarcastically as \"Prime Minister Ambani\" and accused the Indian government of favoring Reliance Jio. India's government has denied bias, noting that Starlink, Jio's satellite venture, and Bharti-backed Eutelsat OneWeb all hold satellite communication licences but await final security clearances and spectrum pricing.\n\nStarlink's published India pricing from December 2025 was ₹8,600 per month plus a ₹34,000 hardware kit, a premium compared to Jio's cheapest plans. The services are not direct substitutes: Jio's mobile data is per-person, while Starlink targets areas without fibre coverage. For Jio investors, the competitive threat is real but untested, and regulatory delays could postpone any impact.",
      "sources": [2, 3, 6]
    }
  ]
}

