  "view": "The ESOP dispute dominates the news. Over 1,000 ex-staff want liquidity on vested options worth roughly $1.9 billion, and Walmart has given no firm IPO timeline. The IPO could slip another one to two years. Festive sale noise is routine and adds little. Net: bearish on clarity.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Ex-Flipkart staff press Walmart on ESOP liquidity",
      "essay": "More than 1,000 former employees of the Flipkart group have appealed to Walmart and Flipkart's board for an exit on their vested stock options. The group spans Flipkart, Myntra, Cleartrip, Shopsy, Supermoney and ANS. A petition dated October 10 gathered over 500 signatures within 24 hours and aims to rally 20,000 to 30,000 former staff.\n\nThree demands: extend the liquidity offered to current employees to eligible ex-employees, extend the post-exit exercise window for vested options, and appoint a point of contact for talks. The move follows an October 1 letter from former senior executives, including ex-Myntra CEO Mukesh Bansal and ex-CFO Sanjay Baweja, seeking \"fair and equitable treatment.\" Walmart's counsel said former employees will be considered at the IPO, without a firm commitment.\n\nMoney is the pressure point. Current and former employees stand to share about $4 billion (roughly Rs 38,000 crore) from buybacks Walmart has not yet run. Former employees account for roughly half, about $2 billion. Flipkart was valued at $38.2 billion in July's buyback, up 6 per cent from $36 billion in May 2024, when eligible staff could sell up to 5 per cent of vested options. The company targets an IPO valuation of about $50 billion. For anyone holding Flipkart exposure, the signal is governance friction: the people who built the company are fighting for the same exit that public-market investors are waiting on.",
      "sources": [2, 3, 4]
    },
    {
      "headline": "IPO timeline stays open as Walmart stays cautious",
      "essay": "Flipkart's listing could slip by another one to two years, with Walmart taking a wait-and-watch approach. Peers have moved: Swiggy and Urban Company already listed, Zepto and Snapdeal have started IPO paperwork, and Meesho tapped the public market. Flipkart has no timeline. CEO Kalyan Krishnamurthy said in July that the company never had one. A roughly $2 billion private round was explored but did not close, and Walmart asked Krishnamurthy to prioritise profitability first.\n\nThe finances show why. Flipkart Internet, the marketplace arm, narrowed its net loss to ₹1,494.2 crore in FY25 from ₹2,358.7 crore in FY24, a 36.7 per cent improvement. Total income rose 14 per cent to ₹20,807.4 crore from ₹18,241.6 crore. Revenue from operations rose 14.4 per cent to ₹20,493.3 crore. Past ESOP programmes have paid out over $1.5 billion to employees. Uncertainty is feeding retention talk, with some staff looking outside the group. For a holder, the delay is the core problem: no listing date means no clean exit and no price discovery.",
      "sources": [3, 4, 2]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Big Billion Days runs, with mixed iPhone deals",
      "essay": "Flipkart's Big Billion Days 2026 sale carries the 13th edition of the event, with early access from October 8. It competes with Amazon's Great Indian Festival. Apple's iPhone 17 Pro is the headline item on both platforms.\n\nFlipkart's iPhone prices have moved during the sale. The iPhone 17 Pro and Pro Max sit at effective prices of ₹1,29,900 and ₹1,44,900. The iPhone 15 is at ₹58,899. The standard iPhone 17 started near ₹80,000, dipped to about ₹75,000 with an EMI discount, then Flipkart raised the effective price to around ₹81,999. Apple lifted iPhone 18 Pro prices because of a memory shortage, with that model starting at ₹1,64,900. Bank offers and exchange bonuses cut the final price further.\n\nThis is normal festive noise. It shows the platform can pull shoppers, but the discounts are not exceptional, and none of it changes the equity story.",
