  "view": "The story is expansion, but the numbers decide: Rs 909 crore net loss in FY26 and a Rs 202 crore loss in the June quarter. The order book is Rs 1,529 crore and 93% domestic. A single 5G deal in South America and one Europe entry do not yet offset a shrinking domestic base after BSNL. Execution risk stays high until international orders translate into revenue.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Tejas Networks plans Europe and Global South expansion as losses widen",
      "essay": "The order book told one story. The earnings told another. Tejas Networks, the Tata-backed telecom equipment maker, is pitching itself as a global challenger. Its chief strategy and business officer, Sanjay Malik, says the company has secured a deal in Europe and is engaging telcos in Africa, Asia and South America. But the company booked a Rs 909-crore net loss in FY26 on revenue of Rs 1,103 crore. In the quarter ended June, revenue was Rs 402 crore with a net loss of Rs 202 crore. The order book stood at Rs 1,529 crore, and 93% of it was domestic. The company that wants to win the Global South is still, for now, an Indian business.\n\nThe domestic anchor is BSNL. Tejas deployed the state carrier's 4G network across more than 100,000 sites from 2023 to early this year. A second purchase order for another 18,000 sites is underway. That work made Tejas a credible builder of end-to-end networks. It also concentrated the revenue. When BSNL slows, the numbers slow with it. Malik's answer is to take the same stack abroad: 2G, 4G and 5G, hardware and software integrated, so customers avoid the layered investments of legacy kit. The pitch is cost. Fewer vendors, lower operating expense, faster deployment.\n\nThe European deal is a door. The South American 5G order, won in the June quarter, is the first commercial end-to-end deployment outside India. Tejas has a presence in 17 countries, but India remains its largest revenue-generating market. The company leans on the Tata group to change that. TCS can act as the systems integrator on contracts, and Tata International can handle trading and distribution. Malik is clear that this is the point of being inside the group: relationships that open meetings, and a global integrator that can carry the solution into markets where Tejas has no history.\n\nThere is a technology bet underneath the sales push. Tejas's CTO and cofounder, Kumar Sivarajan, is vice-chairman of the Bharat 6G Alliance. Malik says the company is part of the standardization body, and expects an early development cycle to let it lead rather than follow Ericsson and Nokia. Tejas is also building network orchestration software with AI technology. That is the long game. The short game is converting a single European deal and a single South American order into a revenue line that matters against a Rs 909-crore loss. My view: the strategy is coherent and the timing is early. The risk is that the order book stays domestic while the losses stay real.",
      "sources": [1]
    }
  ]
}

